Each objective is judged on its own: `better` or `worse` mean its difference exceeds
{{ '%g' | format(comparison_rule.verdict_noises) }} noises (one noise is a standard error of the ratio) in the objective's
direction, and `within noise` means this measurement cannot tell the candidate from its base on it.
The result is `better` when no objective is worse and at least one is better (the candidate
dominates its base), `worse` when any objective is worse (a gain on one objective does not
excuse a loss on another), and `within noise` when every objective is inside its noise. Only a
`within noise` result is undecided. The noise is the task's declared per-run noise when it
declares one; otherwise the spread of the runs held, which is wider the fewer runs there are; and
where there is no spread to read (one run, or runs that all read alike) a default of
{{ '%g' | format(comparison_rule.default_noise * 100) }}% of one run. While a result is within noise, the framework runs the candidate and its
base again itself, up to {{ comparison_rule.min_runs }} runs each when the noise is not declared.
Evaluating unchanged candidate content again starts no new run (only one the machinery
failed is run again) and adds no sample: it returns the evaluation already made with its
comparison as held now, so call it again to read a provisional comparison once the
framework's runs have landed. A comparison describes the runs held now; once a candidate's
official measurement is stored, the value stored then is the one it is ranked by, and later
runs move the comparison, not that value.
